          },
          {
            "section_type": "example_explanation",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Here, <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5]</span> raised to the power of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3]</span> means <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^3.]</span></p><p>To compute this power, we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 3]</span></p><p>Now, we compute the product. Since <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5 \\times 5 = 25,]</span> we have\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5 \u00d7 5_(\u23df)_(25) \u00d7 5|= 25 \u00d7 5.]</span></p><p>Finally, we work out <span class=\"math\">[MATH: {\\color{blue}25} \\times 5.]</span>\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: _(2); 2|5; \u00d7|5; 1|2|5]</span></p><p>Therefore, <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 5^3=125.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Here, [MATH: 5] raised to the power of [MATH: 3] means [MATH: 5^3.]\n\nTo compute this power, we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n[MATH: 3]\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since [MATH: 5 \\times 5 = 25,] we have\n[MATH: 5 \u00d7 5_(\u23df)_(25) \u00d7 5|= 25 \u00d7 5.]\n\nFinally, we work out [MATH: {\\color{blue}25} \\times 5.]\n[MATH: _(2); 2|5; \u00d7|5; 1|2|5]\n\nTherefore, [MATH: 5^3=125.]",
