    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo evaluate a power, we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the resulting product.\n\nContent:\nSo, to find the value of [MATH: 9^2,] we first write it out with multiplication signs as follows:\n[MATH: 2]\n\nContent:\nThen, we compute the product:\n[MATH: 9^{2} = 9 \u00d7 9 = 81]\n\nContent:\nRaising a number to the power of [MATH: 2] is known as squaring a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\nContent:\n- [MATH: 9^2 = 81]\n- The square of [MATH: 9] is [MATH: 81.]\n- [MATH: 9] raised to the second power equals [MATH: 81.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Squaring a Number\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: 4] raised to the second power.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe need to evaluate [MATH: 4^2.]\n\nTo evaluate [MATH: 4^2,] we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n[MATH: 4^{2} = 4 \u00d7 4_(\u23df)_([math]2[/math] times) = 16]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 5^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 10]\nb. [MATH: 7]\nc. [MATH: 25]\nd. [MATH: 52]\ne. [MATH: 55]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the square of [MATH: 9?]The square of [MATH: 9] is\n[FREE_ENTRY 1].\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Tutorial: The Cube of a Number\n\nContent:\nLet's evaluate the following exponent:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2^{\\color{red}{3}}]\n\nContent:\nWe start by writing our exponent as repeated multiplication:\n[MATH: 3]\n\nContent:\nNow, we compute the product.\n\nContent:\nSince [MATH: 2 \\times 2 = 4,] we have\n[MATH: 2 \u00d7 2_(\u23df)_(4) \u00d7 2|= 4 \u00d7 2.]\n\nContent:\nFinally, since [MATH: {\\color{blue}{4}}\\times 2=8,] we have [MATH: 2^3=8.]\n\nContent:\nRaising a number to the power of [MATH: 3] is known as cubing a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\nContent:\n- [MATH: 2^3 = 8]\n- The cube of [MATH: 2] is [MATH: 8.]\n- [MATH: 2] raised to the third power equals [MATH: 8.]